Differentiating Eq.  gives $\begin{aligned}
 \frac{dF}{dt} &= m\ddot{x},\\
 \frac{dF}{dt} &= \frac{dF}{dx}\frac{dx}{dt}
 =F'(x)\dot{x}.\end{aligned}$ Therefore $\ddot{x}=\frac{F'(x)}{m}\dot{x}
 =\frac{F'(x)F(x)}{m^2}.$ Higher derivatives follow by differentiating again. Subject to the usual smoothness assumptions, specifying $x$ at one time fixes the entire local Taylor expansion and hence the motion allowed by this first-order law.

